# SOIVANT Landing — Production Readiness Audit

> Stack: Vite + React 18 + Tailwind CSS 3  
> Type: Pre-launch "coming soon" page with email waitlist capture

---

## 🔴 Critical (Blockers — must fix before going live)

### 1. Waitlist emails are never actually captured
The `submitToWaitlist` function in `WaitlistSection.jsx` is a `setTimeout` stub — it logs to the console and resolves. Every submitted email silently disappears.

**Fix options (pick one):**
- **Resend / Mailchimp / Klaviyo** — fire a `fetch()` POST to their API inside `submitToWaitlist`.
- **Serverless function** — Vite-deployed to Vercel/Netlify with a `/api/waitlist` endpoint that writes to a DB or forwards to an ESP.
- **Getform / Formspree / ConvertKit** — drop-in form services that need zero backend code.

```js
// WaitlistSection.jsx — replace the mock with a real call
async function submitToWaitlist(email) {
  const res = await fetch('/api/waitlist', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ email }),
  });
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error('Submission failed');
}
```

### 2. No CSRF / bot protection on the form
Currently anyone (or any bot) can fire the endpoint repeatedly. Add:
- **Honeypot field** (hidden input — zero-friction for real users, catches naive bots)
- **Rate limiting** at the API/edge level (Vercel edge middleware, Cloudflare rules)
- **hCaptcha / Cloudflare Turnstile** if spam becomes a real issue after launch

### 3. Countdown is hardcoded to local time without timezone
`launchDate = '2026-11-14T09:00:00'` has no timezone suffix. `new Date()` will interpret it as **local machine time**, so the countdown will be off by hours depending on the visitor's locale.

```js
// content.js — always use an explicit offset or UTC
export const launchDate = '2026-11-14T09:00:00+03:00'; // Nairobi / EAT
// OR
export const launchDate = '2026-11-14T06:00:00Z';       // UTC equivalent
```

---

## 🟡 High-Priority (ship as soon as possible)

### 4. Missing Open Graph image (`og:image`)
The `<head>` has `og:title` and `og:description` but no `og:image`. Every social/WhatsApp share will show a blank card. This is critical for a launch that relies on social word-of-mouth.

```html
<!-- index.html -->
<meta property="og:image" content="https://soivant.co/og-image.jpg" />
<meta property="og:image:width" content="1200" />
<meta property="og:image:height" content="630" />
<meta property="og:url" content="https://soivant.co" />
<meta property="og:type" content="website" />
<!-- Twitter/X card -->
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
<meta name="twitter:image" content="https://soivant.co/og-image.jpg" />
```

Generate the OG image at 1200×630px using the brand poster aesthetic.

### 5. Google Fonts loaded via `<link>` — no fallback / FOUT control
Fonts are loaded from Google CDN at runtime. On slow connections, users will see a flash of fallback serif. Since the design leans heavily on Bodoni Moda and Marck Script, this is very noticeable.

**Options:**
- Self-host via `fontsource` npm packages (zero network request, no GDPR concern):
  ```
  npm i @fontsource/bodoni-moda @fontsource/marck-script @fontsource/inter
  ```
  Then import in `main.jsx` instead of the `<link>` tags.
- Or add `font-display: optional` / `font-display: swap` to the Google Fonts URL (already partially handled by `display=swap` in the URL — but self-hosting is still better for perf + privacy).

### 6. No `favicon.ico` / `apple-touch-icon`
The `<head>` has no favicon. Browsers show an ugly generic icon and the tab/bookmark looks unprofessional.

```html
<!-- index.html -->
<link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
<link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png" />
```

Create a minimal "S" or monogram mark as an SVG favicon — it'll look clean at small sizes.

### 7. Stray `{components,data,assets,hooks}` directory in `src/`
There is a literal directory named `{components,data,assets,hooks}` inside `/src/`. This is likely a shell-glob expansion artefact from a bad `mkdir` command. It will be included in the production bundle by Vite's file watcher and confuse any future developer.

```bash
rm -rf "src/{components,data,assets,hooks}"
```

### 8. No `robots.txt` or `sitemap.xml`
Both are expected by search engines and crawlers.

```
# public/robots.txt
User-agent: *
Allow: /
Sitemap: https://soivant.co/sitemap.xml
```

---

## 🟠 Medium Priority (polish & stability)

### 9. WaitlistForm success state ignores `successHeading` / `successBody` from `content.js`
`content.js` already defines `waitlistCopy.successHeading` and `waitlistCopy.successBody`, but `WaitlistForm.jsx` hardcodes its own success string: `"You're on the list — check your inbox soon."`. Pass the copy down as props so marketing can update it from a single source.

### 10. Mobile nav sheet has no animation / trap focus
The mobile menu snaps open/closed with no transition (`open && <div>`). This feels abrupt. It also has no focus trap — keyboard users can tab past the sheet into hidden content behind it.

Minimal fix:
```jsx
// Replace the `{open && <div>}` with a CSS-transition wrapper
<div
  className={`md:hidden overflow-hidden transition-all duration-300 ease-in-out
              ${open ? 'max-h-96 opacity-100' : 'max-h-0 opacity-0'}`}
>
  {/* nav links */}
</div>
```

For full accessibility: add `aria-modal`, use `inert` attribute on the rest of the page, or install a small focus-trap library.

### 11. CollectionPreview product cards have no `alt` text path
The current product "images" are gradient divs (fine for now), but the placeholder comments say "swap for an `<img>` once available." When you do, make sure alt text is part of the product data schema in `content.js` before launch:

```js
// content.js
{ id: 'hoodie-geschichte', ..., alt: 'Geschichte Hoodie in heavyweight fleece — front view' }
```

### 12. `launchDate` countdown will keep ticking after launch — nothing happens
When the date passes, `Countdown` shows "The Geschichte line is live." — but the rest of the page still says "Coming Soon," the product cards are still locked, and the waitlist CTA is still the primary action. The post-launch state needs a design decision: either redirect to the store, swap `locked: false`, or at minimum replace the hero headline.

### 13. No `<meta name="robots">` control while staging
If you deploy to a staging URL before the public launch (e.g. a Vercel preview), search engines may index the staging version. Add a `noindex` meta in non-production builds:

```js
// vite.config.js — expose the env variable
// Then in index.html template or inject via plugin:
// <meta name="robots" content="noindex" />  ← only in staging
```

### 14. Vite config has no `base` path set
If the site is ever deployed to a subdirectory (e.g. `soivant.co/launch/`), all asset paths will break. It's `'/'` by default, which is fine for a root deploy — but should be documented or explicitly set to avoid surprises:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default defineConfig({
  base: '/',   // explicit — document your deployment assumption
  ...
});
```

---

## 🟢 Low Priority / Nice-to-Have

### 15. No scroll-triggered animations
The `animate-fadeUp` class only fires once on load (no `IntersectionObserver`). Sections below the fold (Manifesto, Collection, Waitlist) won't benefit from the entrance animation because they're already in the DOM at load. Consider a lightweight intersection-observer hook or a library like `framer-motion` with `whileInView`.

### 16. No `prefers-color-scheme` consideration
The site is dark-only. That's perfectly fine for the brand, but it's worth an explicit `color-scheme: dark` meta to prevent white flash before CSS loads:

```html
<meta name="color-scheme" content="dark" />
```

### 17. `.eslintrc.cjs` not audited for React 18 / hooks coverage
The ESLint config should have `react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` enabled to catch stale closure bugs — especially in `useCountdown` where `target` is a derived primitive (currently fine, but fragile as the hook grows).

### 18. No `<noscript>` fallback
If JS is blocked, the user sees a blank page. A `<noscript>` tag with a minimal "Coming soon" message is a 2-minute fix:

```html
<noscript>
  <style>body { background: #0B0A08; color: #EDE8DC; font-family: Georgia, serif; padding: 4rem 2rem; text-align: center; }</style>
  <h1>SOIVANT — Coming Soon</h1>
  <p>JavaScript is required to view this page. Follow us on Instagram @soivant.co for updates.</p>
</noscript>
```

### 19. No analytics
No Google Analytics, Plausible, or PostHog instrumentation. You won't know how many people visited, what devices they used, or how far down they scrolled — data you'll want post-launch to evaluate the campaign.

### 20. Bundle size / performance baseline
Run `npm run build` and check the dist output. With no heavy dependencies, the bundle should be very small. Confirm:
- No `node_modules` accidentally imported at runtime
- Fonts are subset (Google Fonts `display=swap` handles this partially, self-hosting gives full control)
- Images (once real product photography lands) go through `vite-imagetools` or are served as WebP

